                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1437-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>Sterling Audio ST33 FET</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Pencil Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The Sterling ST33 is a medium-diaphragm pencil condenser with cardioid pattern and solid-state circuitry.</p><p>The mic body is nickel-plated brass with black finish. The head and grille are stainless steel.</p><p>A 12dB/Octave high pass filter at 75 Hz is switchable on the mic body. A second switch activates a -15dB attenuation pad.</p><p>This Class-A FET mic is based on the Groove Tubes GT-33. Unlike the GT-33, the mic does not have swappable capsules.</p><p><a rel="shadowbox" href="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images//mic_extras/sterling/ST33-case.jpg"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/sterling/ST33-case-sm.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="ST33 FET" width="200" /></a>The mic ships in a padded flightcase with an elastic shockmount (p/n HM-1).<p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Sterling/ST33">Sterling Audio ST33</a></p><p>The mic was released in 2006.</p>
